course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 6
title: Brothers, aunties, cousins and the polite "vy"
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A Ukrainian sim'yA (family) is often big and close: brat and sestrA, cousins who feel almost like brothers, aunties, uncles and godparents at every party.
explain: Many families also use TItko (auntie) and DYAdko (uncle) for close family friends, the way some English kids say «Auntie Sue» to Mum's best friend.
explain: The golden rule for a guest is the little word vy: kids say vy, the polite «you», to the friend's mum, dad, granny and teachers, and keep ty for friends.
explain: Teachers and older adults are called by first name plus father's name — if a teacher is Olena and her father was Petro, she is Olena PetrIvna, and getting that ending right sounds very respectful.
fact: brat means brother, as in tse miy brat, that's my brother; between boys bratan or brO means bro.
fact: sestrA means sister, and sestrychka is a loving word for a little sister.
fact: molOdshyi brat means younger brother, and stArshyi means older.
fact: TItka or TItochka means auntie, and kids also say TItko politely to women friends of the family; calling a strange woman TItka in a shop can sound rude.
fact: DYAdko means uncle or a man friend of the family; DYAdya is more Russian-sounding but common.
fact: dvoYUrIdnyi brat means cousin, a boy cousin, and cousins are very close in many Ukrainian families.
fact: sim'yA means family; the apostrophe marks a little break, seem-YAH.
fact: batkY means parents, as in de tvoyi batky? (where are your parents?), while bAtko is a formal word for father.
fact: vy is the polite «you» to grown-ups; saying ty to the friend's granny is cheeky, and even where kids say ty to their own mum, a guest uses vy.
fact: Teachers and older adults are addressed by first name and father's name, for example Olena PetrIvna.
fact: onUk is grandson and onUka granddaughter, the granny's words for her grandchildren.
fact: khreshchEnyi and khreshchEna mean godfather and godmother; godparents are treated almost as family.
exercise: What does brat mean? | Brother | Naughty child | A cousin | An uncle
exercise: How would Sashko introduce his sister? | tse moya sestrA | tse miy brat | tse moya TItka | tse moya onUka
exercise: What does molOdshyi brat mean? | Younger brother | Older brother | Twin brother | Cousin brother
exercise: What is stArshyi brat? | Older brother | Younger brother | Stepbrother | Grandad
exercise: What does TItka mean? | Auntie | An uncle | A granny | A teacher
exercise: Why might calling a strange woman TItka in a shop go wrong? | It can sound rude to a stranger | It means «old lady» officially, on forms | It is a man's word | It is only for nurses
exercise: What does DYAdko mean? | Uncle, or a man friend of the family | Grandad, Mum's or Dad's father | Godmother, who comes to every christening | Little brother
exercise: Which word is more Russian-sounding but common? | DYAdya | DYAdko | TItka | brat
exercise: What is a dvoYUrIdnyi brat? | A boy cousin | A twin brother | A brother-in-law | A best friend
exercise: What does sim'yA mean? | Family | The number seven | A sister | Your home
exercise: What are batkY? | Parents | Grandparents | Brothers | Teachers
exercise: Which «you» does a guest use to the friend's granny? | vy | ty | tvoya | nE-a
exercise: How would kids address a teacher named Olena whose father was Petro? | Olena PetrIvna | TItka Olena | Petro Olenovych | Olenka
exercise: What does onUka mean? | Granddaughter | Grandmother | Aunt | Niece's dog
exercise: Who are khreshchEnyi and khreshchEna? | Godfather and godmother | Uncle and aunt | Grandad and granny | Brother and sister
exercise: How are godparents treated in many Ukrainian families? | Almost as family | As strangers | As teachers | As neighbours only
test: «brat means a naughty kid, like English» Which is right? | No, brat means brother | Yes, it means a spoilt child | Yes, it is an insult | No, it means cousin
test: You say «ty» to Sashko's granny: «a ty budesh chai?» (will you have tea?). What would happen? | It would sound cheeky; a guest says vy to her | Nothing, ty is the polite form for grannies | She would think you meant the cat in the kitchen | She would answer in English
test: «Sashko calls his own mum ty, so I can too» What is right? | Some families let their kids say ty, but a guest uses vy | Yes, a guest always copies the kids | No, nobody ever says ty to a mum | Yes, ty is polite to all mums
test: Sashko says his dvoYUrIdnyi brat Ivan is coming. Ivan is the son of Sashko's aunt. Who is Ivan to Sashko? | His cousin | His uncle, Mum's brother | His younger brother | His godfather
test: Sashko's mum calls her best friend «Maryna» and Sashko calls her «TItko Maryno». Is Maryna his real aunt? | Not necessarily — kids say TItko to family friends too | Yes, TItko is only for real aunts, Mum's or Dad's sisters | No, TItko means teacher | Yes, TItko means mum's sister by law
test: «sim'ya means seven, sim is seven» Which is right? | No, sim'yA means family; sim alone is seven | Yes, a family of exactly seven people, no more | Yes, it's a number | No, it means summer
test: «de tvoyi batky?» asks a policeman at the fair. What does he want to know? | Where your parents are | Where your bike is | Where your shoes are | Where your brother is
test: The teacher's father was called Ivan and she is Mariya. What would happen if you called her Mariya IvAnivna? | It would sound very respectful | She would think you were rude | She would think you meant her mum | She would correct you to TItko
test: «Granny keeps saying onUk to me, is that my name now?» Which is the best answer? | It means grandson — she treats you like one of hers | It's a nickname for a cat, she thinks you're cute | It means guest, the polite word for a visitor | It means naughty
test: In a cartoon a boy shouts «stArshyi brat!» at his little brother. What is wrong? | stArshyi means older; the little one is molOdshyi | Nothing, stArshyi means little, the youngest in the family | stArshyi means sister | stArshyi is only for uncles
test: «oi bratan, give it here» says a boy to his friend. What is bratan here? | bro, a friendly word between boys | his real brother, born to the same mum | his uncle | an insult
exam: Identify the form of the second-person pronoun that a visiting child is expected to employ in addressing the grandmother of his companion. | vy | ty | tvIy | tvoya
exam: Select the response consistent with the established usage of the first name together with the father's name. | It constitutes the respectful mode of address towards teachers and older adults | It is reserved for addressing younger siblings | It is employed only by grandparents towards their grandchildren upon formal occasions | It is regarded as discourteous in all circumstances
exam: Ascertain the relationship denoted by dvoYUrIdnyi brat. | A male cousin | A younger brother | A godfather | A stepfather
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the word TItka? | It denotes an aunt and may be extended to female friends of the family, whereas addressed to an unknown woman it may appear discourteous | It denotes a grandmother exclusively | It is a formal title for female teachers | It is a word employed only in respect of men
